son of the Duke of Luynes.

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:38.060
"He killed my father. Prepare to die."

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:41.040
Quite the opposite.

00:10:41.049 --> 00:10:43.199
One of his companions came to offer his apologies.

00:10:43.199 --> 00:10:44.220
"He resurrected my father?

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:45.540
"Prepare to live?"

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:53.500
Okay, when I say the opposite, I mean in more
general terms.

00:10:53.500 --> 00:10:57.080
It was more that they certainly weren't enemies
after this.

00:10:57.080 --> 00:10:59.740
Is there more about...
just before we go onto this guy,

00:10:59.749 --> 00:11:01.920
is there more about him, other than this incident?

00:11:01.920 --> 00:11:04.639
Because I would hate to be, myself, one of
those people in history

00:11:04.639 --> 00:11:07.020
that's only remembered as being the guy that
got stabbed through a shoulder,

00:11:07.020 --> 00:11:08.379
and potentially stuck to a tree,

00:11:08.379 --> 00:11:10.019
by that lady that pretended to be a nun,

00:11:10.019 --> 00:11:11.529
who sang opera, that burnt the convent down.

00:11:11.529 --> 00:11:14.369
'Cos that's not a great way to be remembered,
let's face it.

00:11:14.369 --> 00:11:16.939
For one thing, you can't write all that on
just the one tombstone.

00:11:16.939 --> 00:11:18.769
Yeah, both sides.

00:11:18.769 --> 00:11:20.749
There is not another reference in here.

00:11:20.749 --> 00:11:22.949
This appears to be the one reference to the
man in history.

00:11:22.949 --> 00:11:26.540
"How do they remember me?
...oh, you're kidding. Come on!"

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:30.040
If it hadn't been that, he would have been
remembered for being the innately hilarious

00:11:30.049 --> 00:11:32.299
son of the Duke of Loins!

00:11:32.299 --> 00:11:34.989
...I'm probably mispronouncing that.

00:11:34.989 --> 00:11:37.610
I'm choosing to believe you're not!

00:11:37.610 --> 00:11:39.880
It's L-U-Y-N-E-S.

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:42.100
So if there's any French speakers in here
who can…

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:43.500
Loins!.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:44.380
Loins it is then.

00:11:44.380 --> 00:11:45.100
Duke of Loins.

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:47.149
There's no advance from the audience.

00:11:47.149 --> 00:11:49.689
What happened, though, after he recovered?

00:11:49.689 --> 00:11:52.220
And, by the way, and after he sent his apologies?

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:53.459
Oh, good man.

00:11:53.459 --> 00:11:56.949
I imagine he went, "Ow," quite frequently,
for a while.

00:11:56.949 --> 00:11:58.730
Did his golf take some time to recover?

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:00.389
Was there physiotherapy involved?

00:12:00.389 --> 00:12:03.100
Is this back to swording as an artless euphemism?

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:04.100
Did they marry?

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:06.880
You're closer, yes.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:10.910
They never married, but they became lovers,
and, later, lifelong friends.

00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:12.319
How can you...!

00:12:12.319 --> 00:12:15.019
"Oh, remember that time
I stabbed you in the shoulder?"

00:12:15.019 --> 00:12:17.209
"Ah, that's how we met!"

00:12:17.209 --> 00:12:19.309
Works for me.

00:12:19.309 --> 00:12:23.699
I'm going to point out, she is still in her
late teens at this point.

00:12:23.699 --> 00:12:24.699
That's nails.

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:27.080
We are not in the section, yet, that says,
'Adult Life.'

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:30.480
You know when you're at home and you think
to yourself,

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:31.629
"How is my life going?"

00:12:31.629 --> 00:12:33.319
Do you ever hear articles like this and think,

00:12:33.319 --> 00:12:35.179
"I have not achieved enough in life?"

00:12:35.179 --> 00:12:37.360
Well, how old was she?

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:38.360
Late teens?

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:42.129
By 19, at no point had I tried to burn a convent
down with a corpse inside it.

00:12:42.129 --> 00:12:44.790
She is somewhere between 17 and 20 at this
point, but we don't know if-

00:12:44.790 --> 00:12:45.790
Oh, well, that's different.

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:48.600
By 19, I was all about that, yes.

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:51.279
She was hired by the Paris Opera.

00:12:51.279 --> 00:12:54.029
In full knowledge that she was pretty handy
while she was doing it.

00:12:54.029 --> 00:12:55.480
Oh, no, as a doorman!

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:58.300
Can you just imagine when she got appointed
at the opera,

00:12:58.300 --> 00:13:00.920
the CRB check she must've had
when she got in?

00:13:00.920 --> 00:13:02.170
"What do we have?

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:04.670
"Well, a little bit of shoplifting when you
were 12.

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:06.130
"I'm sure that's all done now...

00:13:06.130 --> 00:13:10.859
"Burning down a convent and stealing a body
to burn down said convent, now that was not-"

00:13:10.859 --> 00:13:13.430
She only set the room on fire;
she didn't burn down the entire convent.

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:14.769
Oh, well that's alright then.

00:13:14.769 --> 00:13:18.009
Five years later,
she's still the talk of Paris.

00:13:18.009 --> 00:13:21.920
She's still well known-
thank you for those effects.

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:26.800
At a society ball, there was a scandal.

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:27.800
Turned up as a bloke.

00:13:27.800 --> 00:13:28.800
Left with a woman.

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:29.800
Reverted to being a woman.

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:30.990
Burnt the place down when somebody said, "Oi."

00:13:30.990 --> 00:13:32.790
One of those things is right,

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:36.170
but it was a broad, blunderbuss attempt there.

00:13:36.170 --> 00:13:37.209
So let's try Gary.

00:13:37.209 --> 00:13:38.209
Singing opera.

00:13:38.209 --> 00:13:39.209
Throws sword.

00:13:39.209 --> 00:13:40.209
Stabs man.

00:13:40.209 --> 00:13:41.209
Room on fire.

00:13:41.209 --> 00:13:42.209
None of those.

00:13:42.209 --> 00:13:43.209
So based on those two-

00:13:43.209 --> 00:13:44.209
Come on, Will.

00:13:44.209 --> 00:13:45.360
She pretended to be a doctor.

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:54.020
And she went around the ballroom, going,

00:13:54.020 --> 00:13:56.220
"Everyone here has a terrible disease!

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:57.679
"You must all come with me."

00:13:57.679 --> 00:14:00.660
What I like about it, in my imagination,

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:01.799
she's dressed as an opera singer.

00:14:01.799 --> 00:14:04.470
She's got swords down each leg, so she's a
bit stiff in walking,

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:06.739
and has a stethoscope.

00:14:06.739 --> 00:14:10.350
Chris, you actually said vaguely the right
words there.

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:13.059
She kissed a young woman at a society ball.

00:14:13.059 --> 00:14:14.919
What happened immediately after that?

00:14:14.919 --> 00:14:17.949
She had a duel with the entire society ball.

00:14:17.949 --> 00:14:20.709
Yes, she was challenged to duels by
three separate noblemen,

00:14:20.709 --> 00:14:21.810
so I'll give you a point for that.

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:23.800
What did she do that night?

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:26.140
Er, beat them all in duels.

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:29.970
What was illegal in Paris, at that point?

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:31.389
Duelling more than two people in an evening.

00:14:31.389 --> 00:14:32.589
It was considered greedy.

00:14:32.589 --> 00:14:34.239
Just duelling, in particular.

00:14:34.239 --> 00:14:38.419
So she now has to flee Paris, because she's
also wanted there.

00:14:38.419 --> 00:14:39.709
Mate, I'd say Marseilles now.

00:14:39.709 --> 00:14:40.989
I think we're going there.

00:14:40.989 --> 00:14:43.660
I think she went to Marseilles!

00:14:43.660 --> 00:14:44.740
Brussels.

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:51.980
Re-joins the opera, and the final years of
her career here

00:14:51.989 --> 00:14:56.149
were spent in a relationship with, well, who?

00:14:56.149 --> 00:14:57.799
Anyone brave enough.

00:14:57.799 --> 00:14:59.829
Madame la Marquise de Florensac.

00:14:59.829 --> 00:15:02.639
I'm, again, butchering all the French in here.

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:05.209
Well, that's what she was doing with the swords,
to be honest.

00:15:07.340 --> 00:15:08.980
That's taken time.

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:10.200
That's excellent.

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:12.790
Ugly four that just reached the rope, yeah.

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:14.800
On her death, she was inconsolable.

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:18.020
She retired from the opera, and then took
refuge where?

00:15:18.020 --> 00:15:19.680
Not a convent...?

00:15:22.400 --> 00:15:27.540
She died at the age of 33, has no known grave,
and is a legendary figure.

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:29.749
That window needs just a gentle tap
on it now, doesn't it?

00:15:29.749 --> 00:15:32.929
With a point of a sword, just to come through...

00:15:32.929 --> 00:15:36.700
So at the end of the show,
congratulations Will, you win this one.

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:38.320
Yay!

00:15:40.369 --> 00:15:44.739
You win a Greek philosopher's
Mexican salamander speed control.

00:15:44.739 --> 00:15:46.009
Oh!

00:15:46.009 --> 00:15:49.040
Aristotle axolotl something throttle.

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:50.040
Yes!

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:55.260
An Aristotle axolotl throttle.

00:15:55.260 --> 00:15:57.080
Congratulations, Will.

00:15:57.080 --> 00:15:59.500
With that, we say thank you to Chris Joel,

00:16:01.360 --> 00:16:02.620
to Gary Brannan,

00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:04.200
to Will Seaward.

00:16:04.200 --> 00:16:06.199
I've been Tom Scott,
